WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:12.460
One day, James—our master of microscopes—was
out and about on one of his sampling trips,

00:00:12.469 --> 00:00:16.359
collecting various microbial species from
the waters around Warsaw.

00:00:16.359 --> 00:00:22.470
When he got home and began to observe his
new friends under the microscope, he saw this

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:29.019
cool (but also very, very weird) green corkscrew
twisting around.

00:00:29.019 --> 00:00:35.620
Unsure of what this species was, James reached
out to his mentor Professor Bożena Zakryś.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:38.320
Her response surprised him.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:43.120
Yes, Professor Zakryś did know what this species was.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.380
Because in 1986, she was the one who named it.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:01:00.100
Microbe names are weird and wonderful to dive
into because they can tell us so much about

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:04.460
both the organism and also the person who
named them.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:10.710
For example, they might tell us what details
they found most compelling about the organism.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:13.520
Take this colony of diatoms for example.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.770
What’s the first thing you think of when
you see their formation?

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:21.220
Maybe the fact that they’ve created some
sort of asterix?

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.300
That they’re almost star-like?

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:29.660
Well, if so, it’s probably not a surprise
that whoever named it went with “Asterionella,”

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:34.630
the root of which comes from the Greek word
“asterion,” for star.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:39.670
Same for the person who named “Heliozoa,”
who seems to have been understandably inspired

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:42.820
by its resemblance to the sun.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:49.340
Then you have the less astronomical approach,
like when an Italian biologist bestowed the

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:55.440
name “tardigrade,” which translates to
“slow stepper,” upon the magnificent but

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.530
also, yes, slow-stepping water bear.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:08.280
But sometimes, the story behind a microbe’sname is more human, as in the case of Paramecium sonneborni,

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:15.040
named in 2007 for the late scientist
Dr. Tracy M. Sonneborn, who established many

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.599
of the techniques that shaped paramecium research
for decades.

00:02:19.599 --> 00:02:25.849
For Professor Zakryś, naming this species
was also more of a personal affair.

00:02:25.849 --> 00:02:30.779
She found it while traveling to visit family
in southern Poland, collecting samples from

00:02:30.779 --> 00:02:32.439
ponds along the way.

00:02:32.439 --> 00:02:37.689
She’d spent well over a decade working with
euglenoids, but she’d never seen a species

00:02:37.689 --> 00:02:39.370
like this before.

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:43.120
Nor could she recall reading any descriptions
that matched it.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:49.489
So she did what any scientist does when faced
with a mystery: she dug through the literature,

00:02:49.489 --> 00:02:54.569
searching through old research papers to see
if this little green corkscrew matched any

00:02:54.569 --> 00:02:57.599
description of known euglenoids.

00:02:57.599 --> 00:03:03.569
Identifying microbes is a complicated process--we
dedicated a whole episode to how we do it,

00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:10.200
and we’re aided today by the massive amounts
of information readily available on the internet.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:18.609
But this was 1986, an era of nascent internet
precursors and of the Iron Curtain.

00:03:18.609 --> 00:03:24.670
So when Professor Zakryś needed to identify
a euglenoid, she started with the library

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:26.870
of papers she already had on hand.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:31.359
And if they didn’t contain what she was
looking for, she had to hunt through their

00:03:31.359 --> 00:03:34.809
references to find the titles of papers that
might.

00:03:34.809 --> 00:03:39.230
Today, that would just be a cue to google
the title.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:44.400
But she had to send letters to scientists
and libraries around the world to try and

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:50.299
find someone who could help her, and then
wait to hear back—hopefully with a copy

00:03:50.299 --> 00:03:51.730
of the paper.

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:56.389
This could take months, and the papers she
received were often written in a variety of

00:03:56.389 --> 00:03:58.120
different languages.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:06.519
But her husband’s uncle Janusz Smulkowski
spoke 9 languages—6 of which he taught himself—and

00:04:06.519 --> 00:04:12.120
he would help her parse through and translate
what she read, which included many of the

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:15.359
original descriptions of euglenoid species.

00:04:15.359 --> 00:04:22.030
Janusz Smulkowski passed away shortly before
she found this mystery organism.

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:27.490
And so, when she was satisfied that no one
had described it before, she decided to name

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:29.280
it for him.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.720
She called it Euglena smulkowskiana.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:38.240
Of course, you can’t just go around naming
things and deciding that’s that.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.479
You have to get other scientists on board.

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:46.720
You have to convince them that you’ve actually
found something that no one else has named before.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.379
So Zakryś went about writing a paper that
followed the guidelines established by the

00:04:51.379 --> 00:04:54.270
designated nomenclature committee.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:05:00.819
These guides can often become quite complicated
because, well, microbes are pretty complicated.

00:05:00.819 --> 00:05:05.280
You can’t expect the business of naming
them to be straightforward.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:12.580
For reference, the 1990 revision of the International
Code of Nomenclature of Bacteria required

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:18.210
4 chapters to fully outline the principles
behind bacteria nomenclature.

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:26.080
It also contains a list of 65 rules to follow
when assigning a name, and 10 appendices.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.230
Names, it turns out, are serious business.

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:36.129
But even very long and complex naming procedures
have had to face the plot twist of molecular

00:05:36.129 --> 00:05:42.000
techniques, which have a tendency to reveal
hidden connections between organisms that

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.230
seem unrelated.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:49.970
When Zakryś was naming Euglena smulkowskiana,
she was relying on what she could see, on

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:56.290
the bits of morphology that made it seem more
like Euglena and less like other organisms.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:01.940
But later analysis of the organism’s DNA
would reveal what that initial morphological

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:09.719
categorization couldn’t, that this Euglena
was actually a Phacus—a subset of euglenoid

00:06:09.719 --> 00:06:15.979
species whose name “phacus” means “lentil’
in Greek, which quite nicely describes the

00:06:15.979 --> 00:06:21.740
flattened shape that was thought to define
them until molecular techniques showed up

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:23.949
to reveal deeper connections.

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:30.370
So, taking into account some additional nomenclature
rules, Euglena smulkowskiana eventually became

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:31.759
Phacus smulkowskianus.

00:06:31.759 --> 00:06:38.889
There are services that let you name stars,
which sounds quite enchanting.

00:06:38.889 --> 00:06:45.520
But we imagine that there must be a particular
thrill in getting to name the invisible residents

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:52.770
of the microcosmos, who have their own mysterious
lives and ancient histories.

00:06:52.770 --> 00:07:00.129
When we asked Professor Zakryś how it felt
to name a species, she said that it was exciting.

00:07:00.129 --> 00:07:03.409
But also...it was kind of a normal thing for
her.

00:07:03.409 --> 00:07:10.550
She’s been working with euglenoids for over
40 years, carrying out taxonomical verifications

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:17.020
on the various species that exist, and she’s
never counted how many species she’s named

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:19.509
(or renamed) over that period.

00:07:19.509 --> 00:07:26.410
Well, between all the people involved in making
Journey to the Microcosmos, we have currently

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:28.370
named zero species.

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:36.150
So an uncounted number accumulated over decades
sounds, to us, like a lot.

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:42.740
But it also is fitting that the final tally
is almost a non-factor to someone who has

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:49.569
dedicated their professional career to uncovering
and understanding microbes.

00:07:49.569 --> 00:07:56.150
In our popular understanding of how science
gets done, discoveries often sound like singular,

00:07:56.150 --> 00:07:59.950
dramatic events that trace back through millennia
of human achievement.

00:07:59.950 --> 00:08:05.240
A person scrawls a complicated equation on
a chalkboard.

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:08.500
An apple lands on someone’s head.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:11.500
Someone leaps out of a bathtub shouting “Eureka.”

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:19.780
But discoveries are also just a regular everyday
occurrence, a thing happening out in nature

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:27.199
and in laboratories and offices all over the
place, all the time, sometimes even when you’re

00:08:27.199 --> 00:08:29.819
on a trip to visit family.

00:08:29.819 --> 00:08:37.350
For scientists, discovery is the job, and
like any job, it can be both satisfying and

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:39.480
mundane at the same time.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:46.710
In fact, discoveries are often undramatic,
advancing our knowledge in gradual steps,

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:49.730
like the naming of new species.

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:55.700
But they build on each other and build on
each other, creating an ecosystem of named

00:08:55.700 --> 00:09:05.700
ideas and organisms and mechanisms that is
always expanding and fueling the next discovery.

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:11.660
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:17.400
And thank you especially to all of these people,
our patrons on Patreon who make it possible

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:19.260
for us to make this show.

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:23.230
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes James Weiss, you can check out

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:26.060
jam &amp; germs on instagram.

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:30.520
And if you want to see more from us, there’s
always a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

